# Dockerfile Instructions: Detailed Breakdown

Reference Dockerfile:

```dockerfile
# Stage 1: Build the application
FROM node:20-alpine AS builder
# Set the working directory
WORKDIR /app
# Copy dependency files first to leverage Docker cache
COPY package*.json ./
# Install all dependencies (including devDependencies)
RUN npm ci
# Copy the rest of the application source code
COPY . .
# Build the app (if applicable, e.g., TypeScript or React)
# RUN npm run build

# Stage 2: Run the application
FROM node:20-alpine AS runner
# Set production environment
ENV NODE_ENV=production
WORKDIR /app
# Copy only production dependencies from the builder stage
COPY package*.json ./
RUN npm ci --only=production
# Copy built assets or source code from the builder stage
COPY --from=builder /app/server.js ./server.js
# Use a non-root user for security
USER node
# Expose the application port
EXPOSE 3000
# Start the application
CMD ["node", "server.js"]
```

---

## `FROM`

```dockerfile
FROM <image>[:<tag>|@<digest>] [AS <name>]
```

| Option | Details |
|---|---|
| `<image>:<tag>` | e.g. `node:20-alpine` — pin to a tag; omitting it defaults to `:latest` (avoid in production) |
| `<image>@sha256:<digest>` | Pin to an exact image digest for full reproducibility (safer than tags, which can move) |
| `AS <name>` | Names this stage so later stages can reference it (`COPY --from=builder`) |
| `--platform=<platform>` | e.g. `FROM --platform=linux/amd64 node:20-alpine` — force a specific architecture, useful in multi-arch builds |

This Dockerfile uses two `FROM` lines — this is a **multi-stage build**: stage 1 (`builder`) compiles/installs everything; stage 2 (`runner`) copies only what's needed to run, keeping the final image small.

---

## `WORKDIR`

```dockerfile
WORKDIR /path/to/dir
```

- Sets the working directory for all subsequent `RUN`, `CMD`, `ENTRYPOINT`, `COPY`, `ADD` instructions.
- Creates the directory if it doesn't exist.
- Can be used multiple times; relative paths are resolved against the previous `WORKDIR`.
- No flags — just the path argument. Always prefer absolute paths (`/app`) for clarity.

---

## `COPY`

```dockerfile
COPY [--from=<stage>] [--chown=<user>:<group>] [--chmod=<perms>] <src>... <dest>
```

| Option | Details |
|---|---|
| `--from=<stage>` | Copy from a previous build stage (`--from=builder`) or another image (`--from=nginx:latest`) instead of the build context |
| `--chown=<user>:<group>` | Set ownership of copied files (e.g. `--chown=node:node`) — avoids a separate `RUN chown` layer |
| `--chmod=<perms>` | Set permissions on copied files (e.g. `--chmod=755`) |
| `<src>` | Supports wildcards (`*.json`) and multiple sources; paths are relative to the build context (or the source stage if `--from` is used) |
| `<dest>` | Absolute path, or relative to `WORKDIR` |

Note: `COPY` only moves files from the build context or another stage — it can't fetch URLs or auto-extract archives (that's what `ADD` is for, though `COPY` is preferred for plain file copying since it's more explicit/predictable).

`COPY package*.json ./` copies `package.json` and `package-lock.json` (if present) separately from the rest of the source, so the `npm ci` layer is only invalidated when dependencies actually change (Docker layer caching).

---

## `RUN`

```dockerfile
RUN <command>                     # shell form
RUN ["executable", "param1", ...] # exec form
```

| Form | Behavior |
|---|---|
| Shell form | `RUN npm ci` — runs via `/bin/sh -c`, so shell features (`&&`, `\|`, env var expansion) work |
| Exec form | `RUN ["npm", "ci"]` — runs directly without a shell; no shell expansion, but avoids shell-related quirks |

Useful flags:

| Flag | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `--mount=type=cache,target=<path>` | Mount a persistent cache directory across builds (e.g. npm/yarn cache) without baking it into the image |
| `--mount=type=secret,id=<id>` | Mount a secret (like an npm token) only during this RUN step, so it never lands in a layer |
| `--mount=type=ssh` | Forward an SSH agent socket for private git dependencies |
| `--network=<none\|default>` | Control network access during the build step |

`npm ci` specifically: installs exactly from `package-lock.json` (unlike `npm install`, which can update the lockfile) — faster and more reproducible for CI/Docker builds. `npm ci --only=production` (in the runner stage) skips `devDependencies` entirely.

---

## `ENV`

```dockerfile
ENV <key>=<value> [<key2>=<value2> ...]
```

- Sets environment variables persisted into the final image (visible at both build time and container runtime).
- Can set multiple variables in one instruction (fewer layers) or one per line.
- `ENV NODE_ENV=production` here signals to npm and many Node frameworks to skip dev-only behavior/logging and enable production optimizations.
- Differs from `ARG`: `ARG` values only exist during build and aren't in the final image unless explicitly passed into `ENV`.

---

## `USER`

```dockerfile
USER <user>[:<group>]
```

- Sets the user (and optionally group) that subsequent instructions and the final container process run as.
- Can be a username or numeric UID (`USER 1000`); numeric is more portable across images that may not define that user.
- `USER node` uses the built-in non-root `node` user shipped in the official Node.js images — a security best practice, since running as root inside a container is unnecessary risk if the container is compromised.

---

## `EXPOSE`

```dockerfile
EXPOSE <port>[/<protocol>]
```

- Documents which port(s) the container listens on — **purely informational**, it does not actually publish the port.
- Default protocol is `tcp`; can specify `EXPOSE 3000/udp` if needed.
- Actual publishing happens at `docker run -p <host>:<container>` or in a Compose/Kubernetes service definition.
- Can list multiple ports: `EXPOSE 3000 3001`.

---

## `CMD`

```dockerfile
CMD ["executable", "param1", "param2"]  # exec form (preferred)
CMD command param1 param2               # shell form
CMD ["param1", "param2"]                # as default params to ENTRYPOINT
```

| Form | Behavior |
|---|---|
| Exec form (used here) | `CMD ["node", "server.js"]` — runs directly, no shell wrapper; signals (like `SIGTERM` from `docker stop`) go straight to the process, so it shuts down cleanly |
| Shell form | `CMD node server.js` — runs via `/bin/sh -c`, which becomes PID 1 instead of your app, complicating signal handling |

- Only the **last** `CMD` in a Dockerfile takes effect if there are multiple.
- Overridable at runtime: `docker run my-image node other-script.js` replaces the whole `CMD`.
- Different from `ENTRYPOINT`: if both are set, `CMD`'s values are passed as arguments to `ENTRYPOINT` instead of being a standalone command.

---

## Notes on This Specific Dockerfile

- The commented-out `RUN npm run build` — if this is a TypeScript/React/Next app, a build step would be needed before stage 2, since `COPY --from=builder /app/server.js` implies a plain built JS file, not a project needing compilation.
- Only `server.js` is copied from the builder stage — if the app has other files (routes, views, a `dist/` folder), it would need `COPY --from=builder /app/dist ./dist` etc., or the container will fail at runtime with missing modules.
- `npm ci --only=production` is technically deprecated in favor of `npm ci --omit=dev` in newer npm versions, though `--only=production` still works.
